WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.840
Did you know that the sun 
 beams down more energy in an hour

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:05.860
than the entire world uses in a year?

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:09.260
As the world races to avert
catastrophic climate change,

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:13.300
the sun offers by far the most 
abundant source of clean energy.

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:17.060
And by harnessing it, countries can 
also power economic growth,

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:21.420
expand electricity access, 
 and reduce energy imports.

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:25.960
Over the last decade, solar 
has gone from expensive novelty

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:28.460
to the cheapest, fastest-growing 
 power source on Earth.

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:29.700
But here's the thing.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:33.580
We could squander its potential 
if we don't plan for the future.

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:38.060
Today, solar supplies just two percent 
of the world's electricity.

00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:41.000
And even though it might look 
like solar power could

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.480
keep on growing exponentially,

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:46.080
its rise could very well hit a ceiling,

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:48.020
flattening out in coming decades,

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:51.540
far before it unseats dominant fossil fuels.

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:54.800
In Europe, we're already seeing this slowdown,

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:57.160
and if that happens on a global scale,

00:00:57.160 --> 00:00:59.620
the solar revolution could sputter out.

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:03.440
Preventing that is the point of
my new book, Taming the Sun.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:06.260
I argue that three types of innovation are needed

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:08.280
to unlock solar's full potential.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:10.700
The first innovation is financial.

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:15.460
Right now, solar needs to attract 
trillions of dollars to fuel its rise.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:20.660
But so far, the world's most deep-pocketed
investors have largely sat on the sidelines.

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:24.900
So, the solar industry needs to take a
page out of the playbook from the fossil fuel,

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:26.820
automobile, and mortgage industries,

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:29.320
and bundle together solar projects,

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:33.040
so big institutional investors feel
comfortable buying and trading them.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:35.780
I'm pretty confident the industry 
will figure that one out.

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:39.780
But just as soon as solar gets over 
that funding speed bump,

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:42.320
it could run into a much more serious obstacle,

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:45.480
known as value deflation.

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:48.079
See, all this investment will help the industry

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:50.480
produce and deploy more solar panels,

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:53.940
driving down the cost 
of building a new solar project.

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:55.600
That's the good news.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:59.680
But the bad news is that the value
of the electricity produced by solar

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:02.120
will plunge even faster.

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:05.800
As more solar panels come online,
they'll flood the grid with power

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:08.420
in the middle of the day,
but shut off when the sun sets.

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:11.380
Even though customers will
need power during dinnertime,

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:14.440
the next solar panel will just 
feed them more lunchtime power.

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:16.020
That's not very valuable.

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:18.980
Soon, the value will fall below the cost,

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:22.840
so it won't make economic sense 
to install any more solar panels.

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:25.860
That will halt the momentum of solar's rise.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:30.100
Overcoming this barrier
starts with technological innovation.

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:34.420
Breakthroughs in solar technology
could cause the cost of solar to plunge,

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:37.580
enabling more solar to be deployed economically.

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:43.520
Next-generation technologies, such as perovskites,
already exist in laboratories.

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:48.600
They could transform today's heavy, rigid,
and frankly ugly solar panels

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:52.240
into lightweight, flexible, and colorful coatings

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:55.360
that tomorrow could cover cityscapes around the world.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:03:01.300
Additionally, developing advanced solar thermal
plants could convert the sun's energy into heat,

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:04.440
and use that heat to generate power 24/7,

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:06.300
rather than just at lunchtime.

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:10.320
And one day, artificial leaf technology
could even harness sunlight

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:14.760
to make portable fuels, finally making oil obsolete.

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:18.900
Still, even with these two types of innovation,
solar will need a third

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:22.880
to limit the decline of its value
as more of it's deployed.

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:26.380
And that would be systemic innovation, 
which includes things like:

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:28.180
Continent-spanning power grids

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:32.000
that link sun-drenched deserts to power-hungry cities;

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:35.620
Energy markets that pay for
energy storage and flexible generators

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:39.600
to smooth out the volatile swings of solar power;

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:43.680
And smart software that can turn
electric vehicles into mobile batteries

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:47.440
to resupply the grid once the sun goes down.

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:51.480
These innovative energy systems
would preserve solar's value

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:54.200
by making sure that solar power can be used

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:57.200
no matter when it's produced or how it fluctuates.

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:01.280
Promoting all three kinds of innovation
will require urgent investments

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:03.680
by governments all around the world.

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:06.120
And that needs to start right now.

00:04:06.130 --> 00:04:08.640
If we wait until solar runs out of steam,

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:10.740
it'll be too late to get it back on track.

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:12.400
But if we get this right,

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:14.960
the twenty first century
will finally be the one

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:20.260
in which humankind secures
cheap, clean, and virtually limitless energy

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:22.260
all by taming the sun.

